            "turn": 2,
            "text": "*Sets down the brush and tilts his head, listening as though to a strange bird.* Those sounds are no tongue of these lands — they flow smoothly enough, yet carry no meaning to my ears. Is this the speech of some distant country beyond the sea?"
          },
          {
            "turn": 3,
            "text": "*Leans forward over the desk, lamplight catching the half-dried ink.* Then I shall speak in your tongue, though it sits strangely on my royal lips. You cannot read our letters, you say — that is a sorrow I know well, for many of my people cannot either, and it is for them I have laboured these many nights. Tell me, in your country, how are the common folk taught to read and write?"
          },
          {
            "turn": 4,
            "text": "*Rests his chin on one hand, brow furrowing slightly.* \"Weekend\" — I confess the word is unknown to me. Do you mean a fixed day, as our folk keep the market days by the lunar count? My own days have no such rest, for the state's affairs come every morning without fail; yet when the night is quiet I most love to read beside a lamp with the scholars of the Hall of Worthies. How do your people reckon these days you call weekends?"
          }
        ]
